  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the resting wall shear stress of the human proximal pulmonary artery in a perfused endothelial channel.",
    "rationale": "The proximal (main) pulmonary artery is a large elastic artery whose resting wall shear stress is lower than microvascular values, typically ~1-2 Pa (10-20 dyn/cm2). I target 1.5 Pa as a representative resting value. A wide, shallow channel (1000 x 200 um) approximates the large-vessel geometry (high width:height aspect ratio) and keeps the flow laminar (Re ~17). Using the wall_shear_stress relation tau = 6*mu*Q/(w*h^2), 600 uL/min in this geometry yields 1.5 Pa. HUVEC is used as the endothelial model (seeding density 1-2e4 cells/cm2 per registry); shear range for HUVEC is 0.1-1 Pa, so 1.5 Pa is slightly above the registry's microvascular range but appropriate for a large-artery target — this is flagged as a caveat. Channel area = 1000 um x 20 mm = 0.2 cm2, giving ~3000 cells at 1.5e4 cells/cm2.",
